    "keyNotes": "• Importance of Workshop Safety:\n  - Prevention of accidents, injuries, and loss of life.\n  - Protection of costly tools, machinery, and student projects.\n  - Maintenance of an organized, productive, and hygienic working atmosphere.\n• Personal Protective Equipment (PPE):\n  - Eye Protection: Safety goggles or face shields to guard against flying chips and sparks.\n  - Ear Protection: Ear muffs or plugs against excessive machinery noise.\n  - Respiratory Protection: Dust masks against timber sawdust and chemical fumes.\n  - Hand Protection: Leather gloves for sheet metal; heat-resistant gloves for welding/hot operations.\n  - Body & Feet: Heavy-duty workshop apron/overalls, steel-toe boots (no sandals or barefoot).\n• Workshop Safety Rules:\n  - Never run, play, or horseplay in the workshop.\n  - Keep gangways, walkways, and emergency exits clear of off-cuts and oil spills.\n  - Report any damaged tool, frayed electric cable, or spill immediately to the instructor.\n  - Always clamp or secure workpieces in a bench vice before sawing, chiseling, or drilling.\n• First Aid for Common Workshop Injuries:\n  - Cuts & Bleeding: Apply direct pressure with clean gauze, elevate wound above heart level, dress with sterile bandage.\n  - Burns: Hold affected skin under cool running tap water for at least 10–15 minutes; do NOT apply oils or burst blisters.\n  - Fainting: Lay casualty flat on back, elevate feet 30 cm, loosen tight collars, ensure fresh air.\n• Fire Classification and Extinguishers:\n  - Class A: Ordinary combustibles (wood, paper, textiles) -> Water or Foam extinguisher.\n  - Class B: Flammable liquids (petrol, paint, thinners, oils) -> Foam, Dry Chemical Powder, or CO2.\n  - Class C: Flammable gases (butane, LPG) -> Dry Chemical Powder.\n  - Class D: Combustible metals (magnesium, sodium) -> Special Dry Powder.\n  - Electrical Fires: De-energize source first -> Carbon Dioxide (CO2) or Dry Powder (NEVER water).",
