    "keyNotes": "• Classification of Wood:\n  - Hardwoods: Broad-leaved deciduous trees that produce covered seeds (angiosperms). Denser, heavier, slower-growing, with interlocking cellular structure.\n    * Examples in Ghana: Odum (Iroko), Mahogany, Wawa (Obeche), Sapele, Teak, Dahoma, Asanfina.\n  - Softwoods: Needle-leaved, cone-bearing evergreen trees (conifers / gymnosperms). Faster growing, lower density.\n    * Examples: Pine, Fir, Cedar, Spruce (mostly imported or grown in high-altitude plantations).\n• Tree Growth & Cross-Section Structure:\n  - Pith (Medulla): Soft spongy center of the trunk.\n  - Heartwood (Duramen): Inactive, dense, darker central wood providing structural support and natural decay resistance.\n  - Sapwood (Alburnum): Outer active layer conducting sap/water from roots to leaves; lighter in color, susceptible to pests.\n  - Cambium Layer: Thin microscopic cellular layer where active tree growth takes place.\n  - Inner & Outer Bark: Protects tree against physical damage, desiccation, and fungal attacks.\n  - Annual Growth Rings: Concentric rings showing annual growth rate (1 ring = 1 year).\n• Timber Conversion:\n  - The mechanical sawing of felled tree logs into commercial, marketable lumber sizes.\n  - Plain / Through-and-Through Sawing: Log sliced with parallel longitudinal cuts; cheapest and fastest, but prone to cupping and warping.\n  - Quarter Sawing: Log cut into four quadrants, then sliced radially perpendicular to growth rings; produces dimensionally stable, decorative timber but higher waste.\n• Timber Seasoning:\n  - Controlled reduction of excess moisture content (sap) from green timber to equilibrium moisture content (12–15% for Ghana).\n  - Natural / Air Seasoning: Stacking planks outdoors on level stilts with spacer sticks under roofed shed for 6–12 months. Low cost, but slow and dependent on climate.\n  - Artificial / Kiln Seasoning: Drying timber inside a heated, ventilated, humidity-controlled chamber. Fast (few weeks), kills wood-boring insects, but higher capital cost.\n• Wood Defects:\n  - Natural Defects: Knots (branch bases embedded in trunk), shakes (star, cup, or ring shakes from internal tree strain).\n  - Seasoning Defects: Warping (cupping, bowing, twisting), checks, splits, and case-hardening caused by uneven drying rates.",
