    "keyNotes": "• Classification of Metals:\n  - Ferrous Metals: Contain iron (Fe) as their chief constituent; magnetic, prone to rust (oxidation) when exposed to oxygen and moisture.\n    * Mild Steel (0.15% - 0.3% Carbon): Ductile, malleable, easily welded; used for burglar proofs, car chassis, roofing trusses.\n    * High Carbon Steel (0.6% - 1.5% Carbon): Very hard, can be tempered; used for chisels, files, drill bits, hacksaw blades.\n    * Cast Iron (2% - 4% Carbon): Hard, brittle, excellent compressive strength, self-lubricating; used for engine blocks, machine vice bodies.\n  - Non-Ferrous Metals: Contain NO iron; non-magnetic, corrosion-resistant, high electrical and thermal conductivity.\n    * Aluminum: Extremely light, silvery, highly malleable; used for kitchen cookware, aircraft skins, beverage cans, window frames.\n    * Copper: Reddish-brown, supreme electrical and thermal conductor, ductile; used for electric wiring, water pipes.\n    * Lead: Dense, heavy, soft, low melting point; used for battery terminals, radiation shielding, roof flashing.\n    * Zinc: Bluish-white, anti-corrosive; used as galvanic coating on corrugated iron roofing sheets (galvanized iron).\n• Alloys (Combination of Two or More Metals):\n  - Brass: Copper (65–70%) + Zinc (30–35%). Corrosion-resistant, acoustic resonance; used for door handles, musical trumpets, plumbing valves.\n  - Bronze: Copper (88–90%) + Tin (10–12%). Tough, wear-resistant; used for medals, ship propellers, bearings, statues.\n  - Stainless Steel: Iron + Chromium (18%) + Nickel (8%). Does not rust; used for surgical scalpels, kitchen sinks, cutlery.\n  - Solder: Tin (60%) + Lead (40%) or lead-free alloys. Low melting point (approx 185°C); used for joining electrical circuit joints.\n• Mechanical Properties of Metals:\n  - Ductility: Ability of a metal to be drawn into thin wires without breaking (e.g. Copper).\n  - Malleability: Ability of a metal to be hammered or rolled into thin sheets without cracking (e.g. Gold, Aluminum).\n  - Hardness: Resistance of a surface to indentation, scratching, and wear.\n  - Tensile Strength: Resistance of a material to being pulled apart under tension.\n  - Brittleness: Tendency to fracture or snap with minimal plastic deformation when subjected to shock (e.g. Cast Iron).",
