    "id": "jhs1-ct-t10-design-process",
    "subjectId": "career-tech",
    "level": "JHS 1",
    "term": 2,
    "orderIndex": 10,
    "title": "The Design Process and Problem Solving",
    "description": "Systematic design methodology: problem identification, design brief, research, specification, idea generation, prototyping, and evaluation.",
    "isFreeTrial": true,
    "isVip": false,
    "youtubeUrl": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Fj2sXv0u-0g",
    "youtubeId": "Fj2sXv0u-0g",
    "keyNotes": "• What is Design?\n  - A systematic, iterative problem-solving activity aimed at meeting human needs, solving functional challenges, and creating sustainable products.\n• Sequential Stages of the Design Process:\n  1. Identification of the Problem / Situation: Observing an existing difficulty or unfulfilled community need (e.g. students lack a hygienic place to keep school bags off muddy classroom floors).\n  2. The Design Brief: A short, clear statement stating what is to be designed and made, without detailing the final solution. Example: 'Design and manufacture a portable, lightweight, foldable rack to keep six school bags elevated off the floor.'\n  3. Research and Investigation: Gathering information on existing similar products, ergonomics (human dimensions), available materials (wood, PVC, bamboo), tools, and target user preferences.\n  4. Design Specification: A list of measurable requirements the product MUST fulfill (e.g. must cost less than GHS 80, support 30 kg, resist water, fit under classroom windows).\n  5. Generating Possible Ideas: Freehand sketching of diverse creative concepts (quick brainstorming of 3–4 distinct designs).\n  6. Development of the Chosen Design: Combining the best features into one final refined proposal with detailed dimensions, joint selections, and material lists.\n  7. Prototyping / Realization: Building a scale model or full-size working product in the workshop according to working drawings.\n  8. Testing and Evaluation: Testing the completed artifact against the original design specification and gathering user feedback for future improvement.",
    "examples": [
      {
        "id": "ex-ct-des-1",
        "title": "Formulating a Clear Design Brief",
        "problem": "In a JHS classroom, books on the teacher's table are frequently disarranged and blown about by wind. Write a valid design brief.",
        "stepByStepSolution": [
          "Step 1: Identify the root problem: lack of an organized desktop containment system for instructional books.",
          "Step 2: Avoid prescribing a single narrow solution (e.g. do NOT say 'make a box with five nails').",
          "Step 3: Write a concise functional brief: 'Design and produce a compact, aesthetically pleasing desktop book organizer that keeps up to 15 exercise books upright, stable, and easily accessible.'"
        ],
        "keyTakeaway": "A design brief states the problem and scope clearly without pre-judging the specific technical solution."
      },
      {
        "id": "ex-ct-des-2",
        "title": "Evaluating a Prototype against Specifications",
        "problem": "A prototype student book rack breaks when loaded with 8 textbooks. How does the designer use this outcome?",
        "stepByStepSolution": [
          "Step 1: Check specification: the requirement demanded supporting at least 15 kg without joint deflection.",
          "Step 2: Diagnose structural failure point: the butt joints sheared due to lack of mechanical interlock.",
          "Step 3: Re-enter development stage: substitute butt joints with housing joints or corner dowel reinforcements and re-test."
        ],
        "keyTakeaway": "Evaluation reveals structural deficiencies, driving iterative refinement until specifications are met."
      }
    ]
