    "title": "Food Preparation, Cooking Methods, and Kitchen Hygiene",
    "description": "Reasons for cooking food, moist and dry heat cooking methods, kitchen sanitation, prevention of foodborne diseases, and traditional preservation methods.",
    "isFreeTrial": true,
    "isVip": false,
    "youtubeUrl": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Fj2sXv0u-0g",
    "youtubeId": "Fj2sXv0u-0g",
    "keyNotes": "• Reasons for Cooking Food:\n  - Destroys harmful pathogenic bacteria and parasites, making food safe to consume.\n  - Softens tough plant cellulose and animal collagen, making food easily digestible.\n  - Improves flavor, aroma, texture, and visual appetizing appearance.\n  - Extends shelf life and preserves seasonal food abundances.\n• Methods of Cooking:\n  - Moist Heat Cooking Methods:\n    * Boiling: Cooking in boiling liquid at 100°C (e.g. yam, plantain, rice).\n    * Steaming: Cooking food in steam rising from boiling water without direct contact (e.g. abolo, kenkey). Retains delicate water-soluble vitamins.\n    * Stewing: Slow simmering in a small amount of liquid in a covered pot over low heat.\n  - Dry Heat Cooking Methods:\n    * Baking: Cooking by dry convective heat inside an enclosed oven (e.g. bread, meat pies, cake).\n    * Roasting: Cooking uncovered over open charcoal heat or in an oven with hot air and basting fat (e.g. roasted plantain/kofi brokeman, roasted chicken).\n    * Grilling: Cooking food directly on an open gridiron over radiant glowing charcoal heat.\n  - Frying in Hot Fat/Oil:\n    * Shallow Frying: Cooking food in a small amount of fat in a shallow pan (e.g. pancakes, eggs).\n    * Deep Frying: Submerging food completely in hot oil at 170–190°C (e.g. bofrot, fried plantain/kelewele).\n• Kitchen Sanitation and Food Hygiene:\n  - Personal Hygiene: Wash hands thoroughly with soap under running water before touching food, cover hair with a chef cap, wear a clean apron, keep fingernails short.\n  - Cross-Contamination: Transfer of harmful microorganisms from raw food (especially raw meat/poultry) to ready-to-eat cooked food. Use separate chopping boards.\n  - Food Danger Zone: 5°C to 63°C, where foodborne bacteria multiply rapidly. Keep hot food hot (above 63°C) and cold food cold (below 5°C).\n• Traditional Ghanaian Food Preservation:\n  - Smoking: Suspending fish or meat over smoky wood fires (e.g. smoked herrings, catfish). The smoke deposits anti-microbial phenols while drying moisture.\n  - Sun Drying: Spreading pepper, cassava chips (kokonte), or okro on raised mats under direct solar radiation.\n  - Salting: Packing fish (koobi, momoni) in coarse sea salt. Salt draws out moisture by osmosis, inhibiting bacterial growth.",
