    "keyNotes": "• Essential Sewing Tools and Equipment:\n  - Measuring Tools: Flexible plastic tape measure (150 cm / 60 inches) for body measurements.\n  - Marking Tools: Tailor's chalk (wax or chalk cake in various colors) and tracing wheel used with carbon paper.\n  - Cutting Tools: Dressmaker's shears (large angled handles for flat cutting of fabric), embroidery scissors (small sharp tips), and seam ripper (unpicking mistaken stitches).\n  - Sewing & Holding Tools: Hand sewing needles (sharps, betweens), dressmaker's pins, pin cushion, and thimble (protects middle finger when pushing needle through tough cloth).\n• Classification of Basic Hand Stitches:\n  - Temporary Stitches: Held in place temporarily during garment construction and removed after permanent sewing:\n    * Even Basting / Tacking: Equal stitches and spaces (approx 6 mm) along straight seam lines.\n    * Uneven Basting: Long stitch on upper fabric and short stitch underneath for holding wide fabric layers.\n    * Tailor's Tacks: Thread loops cut apart to transfer pattern markings symmetrically to two fabric layers.\n  - Permanent Stitches: Remain permanently in the finished garment:\n    * Running Stitch: Fine, even in-and-out stitches used for gathering, mending, and delicate seams.\n    * Backstitch: Strongest hand stitch; needle enters the fabric behind the previous stitch. Mimics a sewing machine straight stitch.\n    * Hemming Stitch: Slanting neat stitches that secure folded hems to the garment body invisibly on the right side.\n    * Overcasting Stitch: Diagonal loops sewn over raw fabric edges to prevent fraying and unravelling.\n  - Decorative Stitches: Chain stitch, satin stitch, stem stitch, French knots for surface embroidery.\n• Classification of Textile Fibers:\n  - Natural Fibers:\n    * Plant / Cellulose Fibers: Cotton (breathable, absorbent, strong when wet; burns with paper odor and gray ash), Linen (from flax plant; crisp, cool, wrinkles easily).\n    * Animal / Protein Fibers: Wool (from sheep fleece; warm, flame-resistant, shrinks under hot agitation), Silk (from silkworm cocoon; lustrous, soft, luxurious).\n  - Synthetic / Man-Made Fibers:\n    * Synthesized chemically from petrochemical polymers: Nylon, Polyester, Acrylic.\n    * Characteristics: High tensile strength, wrinkle-resistant, quick-drying, hydrophobic, melts into a hard plastic bead when burned.",
